  { title: 'One integration reaches many sites.', text: `A TollBit buyer uses one key, one card and one search across the network. TollBit's homepage says "over 10,000 sites", up from the "nearly 7,000" its CEO cited in April 2026 (${a(src(56), 'TollBit')}, vendor's figures). A FORA agent opens an account at each Exchange it buys from (${a(src(4), 'What is FORA?')}).` },
  { title: 'Blocking bots on sites FORA isn\'t installed on.', text: `Cloudflare can refuse a crawler on any site it fronts, and says "More than 20% of the web sits behind Cloudflare’s network" (${a(src(36), 'Cloudflare')}). FORA's edge function refuses unlicensed AI bots on sites that install it, and nowhere else.` },
  { title: 'Less to install.', text: `Google, CCC, Microsoft and Perplexity work through a contract, a portal or Search Console, with no paywall on your CDN. Google and Perplexity need their crawlers left unblocked. TollBit changes nothing on your origin or CMS. FORA needs its edge function on your CDN and its file on your site (see ${a('#fora', 'What FORA takes')}).` },
  { title: 'Paying by how much your content shaped the answer.', text: `Google's contribution pilot pays when content "contributes significantly"; ProRata scores each source's share of an answer; Parallel says it pays by each source's estimated contribution ${cite(11, 80, 85)}. FORA cannot: the charge is fixed at the sale, and "a usage report never re-prices a purchase" (${a(src(5, '#metering-modes'), 'Licensing terms')}).` },
  { title: 'Seeing which bots visit before you sell.', text: `TollBit and Cloudflare show which bots fetch what ${cite(31, 37)}. FORA Server records transactions and makes no bot-traffic report. It's open source, so an operator can add a log of what agents search for and what its Exchange offers them.` },
  { title: 'A warranty in the license.', text: `TollBit's ${a(src(26), 'standard license')} has you warrant "all rights necessary to provide the Site Content" and indemnify the buyer. FORA cannot state either as a term. A term's obligations are all the buyer's, so a warranty fits only in a license document the term points to (${a(src(5, '#obligations'), 'Licensing terms')}).` },
  { title: 'Payment and bot identity come from other standards.', text: `x402 and MPP settle per request, and Web Bot Auth was first drafted at Cloudflare ${cite(101)}. FORA defines no payment rail, by design, and its agents sign their requests with Web Bot Auth.` },
];

// The eight questions: "Others:" and "FORA:" on separate lines.
const qa = (others: string, fora: string) => `<span class="qa"><span class="qa-who">Others:</span> ${others}</span><span class="qa"><span class="qa-who qa-fora">FORA:</span> ${fora}</span>`;
const QUESTIONS = [
  { title: 'How do you say what\'s for sale?', text: qa(
    `most channels hold your terms in a dashboard or a contract. Supertab and ILC use RSL, and AWS points to an RSL file ${cite(21, 42, 64)}. Cloudflare's Content Signals and the <code>__usage_ai-training</code> group in <code>robots.txt</code> state a preference and no price ${cite(102)}. Anthropic, OpenAI, Google and Microsoft committed to honor that group ${cite(103, 104, 105, 106)}.`,
    `your FORA file names the Exchanges that sell your content, and each holds your terms per page. FORA's ${a('/resources/ai-crawlers/', 'rules generator')} writes the training group. The ${a('/resources/standards-map/', 'standards map')} compares them all.`,
  ) },
  { title: 'How does a buyer find your content?', text: qa(
    `each channel runs its own index under its own rules: TollBit's search, Cloudflare's list of charging domains, Dappier's and Valyu's search. Coinbase's x402 Bazaar ranks by relevance and by "recent call volume and unique payers", and doesn't publish the weights (${a(src(76), 'Bazaar')}). Google, ProRata and Perplexity pick sources inside their own products.`,
    `from your FORA file and your Exchange, or through a Broker that orders offers by relevance (see ${a('#broker-order', 'How a Broker orders offers')}). No index spans every FORA site.`,
  ) },
];
const QUESTIONS_REST = [
  { title: 'Who is the buyer, provably?', text: qa(
    `most use an account and an API key; TollBit and MonetizationOS also match user agents. Cloudflare, AWS and Akamai check Web Bot Auth signatures, and Parallel's crawlers sign with it ${cite(44, 57, 58, 86)}.`,
    `a signature on every request, checked against a key on the buyer's own domain. Cloudflare's bot verification doesn't recognize a FORA agent: it "will fail to verify a message if" it uses the later drafts' header form, which FORA's SDKs send (${a(src(57), 'Cloudflare')}). FORA's edge function checks the signed link itself, and your Cloudflare rules must let signed requests through.`,
  ) },
  { title: 'What lets a given use happen?', text: qa(
    'a pass only TollBit can check; a signed price header Cloudflare checks; an x402 payment AWS verifies; an API key.',
    'a signed link that expires and can be bound to the agent\'s key. Any edge holding the Exchange\'s public key can check it.',
  ) },
  { title: 'Who sets the price, in what unit?', text: qa(
    'you, at bot paywalls, Dappier, Cashmere, Snowflake and CCC\'s per-order sales. The channel itself, at answer engines, Google, Valyu, AskNews and Factiva. The buyer, in Pay Per Use. Units run from a page to a query, a summary, a share or a year.',
    `you, per term. A term is free, flat or per unit, limited by use, country and buyer type, with caps (${a(src(5), 'Licensing terms')}). FORA has no revenue-share price. A buyer that already pays you a share under a contract buys under a deal term (${a(src(5, '#revenue-share-arrangements'), 'Revenue share')}).`,
  ) },
  { title: 'How does money move?', text: qa(
    'TollBit, Cloudflare, CCC and Snowflake collect from the buyer and pay you; Google pays you from its own programs; the rails move money per request.',
    FORA_CELLS.money,
  ) },
  { title: 'What do you see?', text: qa(
    `TollBit and Cloudflare show bot traffic ${cite(31, 37)}; AWS lists each settlement; Google shows one monthly earnings number; ProRata sends monthly statements per article.`,
    'each sale, in the publisher console your Exchange operator runs. No protocol call returns your sales.',
  ) },
  { title: 'What if the numbers are wrong?', text: qa(
    'TollBit and Cloudflare bill only on a delivered page and document no dispute call; ProRata grants an audit right.',
    FORA_CELLS.disputes,
  ) },
];

const SETUP = [
  { title: 'Register your domain.', text: `On the publisher console, name your domain and the paths you license, then choose your terms (${a(src(1), 'Publisher onboarding')}).` },
  { title: 'Serve your FORA file.', text: 'Serve it unchanged on your site. A later change to it needs no redeploy.' },
  { title: 'Add the edge function.', text: 'The console builds it for Cloudflare or CloudFront, and it sits beside what your CDN already runs.' },
  { title: 'Let two kinds of request through your firewall.', text: `Signed requests, and requests for your FORA file, must get past your WAF and bot product (${a(src(3, '#keeping-cdn-bot-protection-on'), 'Keeping CDN bot protection on')}).` },
  { title: 'Keep a sitemap.', text: 'The operator imports your pages from it.' },
];

const BUY_STEPS = [
  { text: 'You set your terms with your Exchange, and your FORA file names the Exchanges that sell your pages.' },
  { text: `An AI agent signs every request with one key, published on its own domain. An assistant on MCP gets one from FORA's ${a(src(6), 'Identity Service')}.` },
  { text: 'The agent asks a Broker, with a list of URLs or in plain words. For each URL, the Broker reads your FORA file and asks one of your Exchanges that it trusts.' },
  { text: 'Your Exchange answers with offers it signed, each carrying your terms and price.' },
  { text: 'The Broker checks every signature and returns the offers, grouped by URL. The agent chooses.' },
  { text: `The agent signs one acceptance over every offer it picks, across Exchanges. The Broker splits the purchase per Exchange (${a(src(7, '#buying-through-the-broker'), 'Broker')}).` },
  { text: 'Each Exchange charges the agent\'s own account and issues a signed link. The agent fetches the page from your CDN, which checks the link. The Broker never charges anyone and never sees the content.' },
  { text: `Your Exchange operator pays you under your agreement. ${SEE_ABOUT}` },
];

const SELL_THROUGH = [
  { title: 'Marketplaces with their own catalog.', text: `A retrieval marketplace, a collective or a rights agency can run a FORA Exchange and Broker over its own catalog and keep its search (${a(src(8), 'For content marketplaces')}).` },
  { title: 'Buyers that pay a share or a fee.', text: `An AI company that pays you a share or a fee under a deal could buy as a FORA agent, under a deal term (${a(src(5, '#revenue-share-arrangements'), 'Revenue share')}).` },
  { title: 'Rights agencies and rails.', text: 'A CCC order maps almost field for field to a FORA term; CCC documents no buyer API. An Exchange operator can collect over x402 or fund an agent\'s account through Skyfire; settlement stays outside the protocol.' },
];

const TODO = [
  { title: 'Ask who has paid.', text: 'Ask each channel for named buyers and the totals they paid.' },
  { title: 'Ask for the fee.', text: `TollBit's, Cloudflare's and Microsoft's aren't published. Ask what a buyer pays in total and what you keep. FORA itself is free, open-source software; for the fee at FORA\'s publisher console, see ${ABOUT}.` },
  { title: 'List what you hand over.', text: 'A subdomain, your logs with your readers\' requests in them, a copy of your archive, a CDN rule. Ask how many engineer-days each one takes.' },
  { title: 'Keep one price per page.', text: 'If a page is on sale in two places, know both prices.' },
  { title: 'Order your CDN rules.', text: 'Let each partner\'s fetcher and the search crawlers through, and keep each paywall\'s rule off the others\' paying buyers.' },
  { title: 'Sell to agents as well.', text: 'Register with an Exchange operator, serve your FORA file and install the edge function on Cloudflare or CloudFront. Agents can then buy under your terms beside every channel above.' },
];

const LEGAL = [
  { title: 'Exclusivity.', text: `Check whether a grant includes training, and whether it is exclusive. An exclusive training grant stops you selling training anywhere else. ProRata's MSA is non-exclusive (${a(src(80), 'ProRata MSA')}).` },
  { title: 'Room for another channel.', text: 'Microsoft\'s PCM runs beside FORA on one site only if your PCM contract allows it.' },
  { title: 'A record of each FORA sale.', text: `With each purchase the buyer signs an acceptance of your terms, and the publisher console can check that signature (${a(src(1, '#after-setup'), 'Publisher onboarding')}).` },
];

// Deep pages: the series' pages (their index summaries), with the collective
// page from the Resources list, in the copy's order.
const collective = RESOURCES.find((r) => r.href === '/resources/transparent-collective/');
const series = SERIES.filter((p) => p.href !== HREF);
const DEEP = [
  ...series.filter((p) => p.href.startsWith(HREF)),
  ...(collective ? [collective] : []),
  ...series.filter((p) => !p.href.startsWith(HREF)),
].map(({ href, title, summary }) => ({ href, title, summary }));

const updated = resourceUpdated(HREF) ?? SERIES.find((p) => p.href === HREF)?.updated;
---
<LandingLayout
  title="Content marketplaces and FORA — FORA resources"
  description="Who pays publishers for AI use today, on what terms, and what each channel asks of your team. Where each is ahead of FORA, and how FORA fits beside them."
>
  <AudienceHero
    current="resources"
    badge="Resources · Content marketplaces"
    title="Every marketplace has its own buyers, units and rules. <em>FORA is the open protocol anyone can run one on.</em>"
    sub={`You're pitched a new way to get paid for AI use every month: a bot paywall, a share of an answer engine's revenue, a Google pilot, a dataset deal, a collective. This page puts the ones already running side by side: who pays, who sets the price, what your engineers change, and where each is ahead of FORA. ${POSITIONING} You sell through one, several or your own, beside the deals you already have.`}
    updated={updated}
  >
    <a href="#map" class="btn btn-primary">See the map</a>
    <a href="#fora" class="btn btn-ghost">What FORA takes</a>
  </AudienceHero>

  <Band label="The short answer">
    <CardGrid items={SHORT_ANSWER} />
    <div class="defs">
      <div><Callout kind="define" {...DEFINITIONS.exchange} /></div>
      <div><Callout kind="define" {...DEFINITIONS.operator} /></div>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band tone="near" id="fora" label="At a glance" title="What FORA takes, for your revenue team and on your stack" lead={`The setup steps are further down, in ${a('#together', 'What changes on your CDN')}.`}>
    <ForaGlance cards={GLANCE} />
    <div class="defs">
      <div><Callout kind="define" {...DEFINITIONS.edgeFunction} /></div>
      <div><Callout kind="define" {...DEFINITIONS.signedLink} /></div>
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" compact id="paid" label="Who has shown money moving">
    <KeyFacts items={KEY_FACTS} />
    <p class="prose after-facts">The map's "Who pays today" column has the rest, in each channel's own words.</p>
  </Band>

  <Band id="map" label="The map" title="Who pays for AI use of your content, and on what terms">
    <MarketMap columns={MAP_COLUMNS} fora={MAP_FORA} groups={MAP} label="Who pays for AI use of your content, and on what terms" />
    <p class="prose note-read"><b>Reading note.</b> The rows compare channels built for different jobs: a rail carries no license, and a collective doesn't set your price. "Who pays today", "Who sets the price" and "What your engineers change" compare like for like. A dash means the channel has nothing to state there. Figures from a vendor are the vendor's own and directional.</p>
    <CardGrid items={RELATIONS} />
    <p class="prose">Court settlements price no future use, so they fit none of the three.</p>
    <Callout kind="define" {...DEFINITIONS.dealTerm} />
    <p class="prose" set:html={`<b>Announced, not running.</b> Next Net and Sundial's SAIL ledger (2026-07-14) names no buyer and publishes no specification ${cite(99)}. AWS hasn't announced the content marketplace The Information reported in February 2026 ${cite(100)}.`} />
    <details class="more">
      <summary>What your engineers change, in full</summary>
      <ul class="more-list">
        {ENGINEERS_FULL.map((it) => <li><b>{it.title}</b> <Fragment set:html={it.text} /></li>)}
      </ul>
    </details>
  </Band>

  <Band tone="near" label="How each kind works" title="Seven ways to sell future use, and one payment for the past">
    <MoneyTiles label="Seven channels and the courts: how money reaches a publisher, and FORA's" tiles={MONEY_TILES} />

    <h3 class="sub-h">Bot paywalls: the bot pays at your door</h3>
    <p class="prose" set:html={`At TollBit, your CDN sends AI bots to <code>tollbit.news.example.com</code>, which TollBit runs. By default a bot without TollBit's pass gets HTTP 402, "payment required". The buyer pays your rate plus "a small transaction fee on top of the rates you set", whose size isn't published (${a(src(27), 'Setting rates')}).`} />
    <p class="prose" set:html={`Cloudflare's Pay Per Crawl, a closed beta, charges "for each successful content retrieval" at your price (${a(src(38), 'Set a price')}). In Pay Per Use, a beta since 2026-09-30, "A buyer offers a price for a specific use of your content" (${a(src(35), 'Pay Per Use')}). AWS WAF's Monetize action answers a matched request with an x402 price, and AWS says what that price leaves out:`} />
    <PullQuote
      quote="AI traffic monetization tells agents how much to pay but not what they're allowed to do with the content."
      who="AWS"
      source="AWS WAF developer guide"
      href={src(42)}
    />
    <p class="prose" set:html={`Fastly and Akamai hand the sale to partners. MonetizationOS's docs describe no way for a bot to pay; its MPP demonstration ran on "a fictional financial data site" ${cite(48, 49)}. Vercel and Netlify can block AI bots, and Google Cloud Armor and Azure Front Door can block bots in general; none of the four charges them ${cite(50, 51, 52, 53)}.`} />
    <Callout kind="caution" points={CAUTIONS} />
    <p class="prose" set:html={`Deep pages: ${a('/resources/content-marketplaces/tollbit/', 'TollBit')}, ${a('/resources/content-marketplaces/cloudflare/', 'Cloudflare')}.`} />

    <div class="kinds">
      {KINDS.map((k) => (
        <section class="kind">
          <h3 class="kind-h">{k.title}</h3>
          {k.paras.map((p) => <p class="prose" set:html={p} />)}
        </section>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" id="ahead" label="Where they are ahead" title="What these channels do that FORA doesn't">
    <PointList tone="neutral" items={AHEAD} />
  </Band>

  <Band label="The differences, question by question" title="Eight questions to ask of any channel" lead="The deep pages ask the same eight questions, in the same order, in their tables.">
    <PointList tone="neutral" items={QUESTIONS} />
    <Callout kind="define" {...DEFINITIONS.broker} />
    <PointList tone="neutral" items={QUESTIONS_REST} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="near" id="together" label="Running FORA beside the others" title="What changes on your CDN, beside TollBit and Cloudflare" lead="Your firewall and bot rules run first, and they must let signed requests and TollBit's fetcher through. Behind them, FORA's edge function decides.">
    <Split aside="460px">
      <p class="prose" set:html={`<b>Beside TollBit.</b> TollBit says another marketplace can run "in parallel" (${a(src(32), 'TollBit')}). Let TollBit's fetcher through FORA's edge, and keep TollBit's rule off requests that carry a FORA signed link. You write that order; FORA ships no combined rule.`} />
      <p class="prose" set:html={`<b>Beside Cloudflare.</b> FORA's edge runs as a Worker after Cloudflare's own rules.`} />
      <Figure
        slot="aside"
        src={`${IMG}/hub-3.png`}
        title="One request through your CDN."
        caption="Your WAF and bot rules run first. For your engineers."
        alt="Five kinds of request reach your WAF and bot rules, which run first. Behind them FORA's edge checks signed links: a reader and Googlebot, Bingbot or Applebot (always passed, never charged) pass to your origin, a FORA agent's signed link is checked and passes, TollBit's fetcher is allowed at the wall and let through the edge, and an AI bot without a signed link is refused on licensed paths."
      />
    </Split>
    <Callout kind="engineers">
      <StepList fora items={SETUP} />
      <p set:html={`Without a console, you can deploy the same edge function yourself (${a(src(2), 'Edge function deployment')}).`} />
    </Callout>
    <Callout kind="search" id="search-traffic" text={SEARCH_FORA.text} links={SEARCH_FORA.links}>
      <p set:html={`Google sets one rule here: "Google doesn't use the content from URLs that return 4xx status codes" (${a(src(19), 'Google')}). No edge, FORA's included, can charge Googlebot and keep the page in Search. Google's and Microsoft's AI uses stay under their own controls.`} />
    </Callout>
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" id="buy" label="When an AI company buys from you" title="What happens when an AI company buys from you through FORA" lead="Each channel above gives a buyer its own account, key and API. A FORA agent also opens an account at each Exchange it buys from, but keeps one signing key and signs one acceptance per purchase." asideWidth="440px" asideAlign="start">
    <StepList slot="intro" fora items={BUY_STEPS} />
    <Figure
      slot="aside"
      src={`${IMG}/hub-2.png`}
      title="One agent, one key, one signed acceptance."
      caption="The same purchase from the agent's side."
      alt="An AI agent with one signing key asks a FORA Broker with URLs or plain words; the Broker reads your fora.json, asks Exchanges A and B for offers, checks every signature and returns the signed offers grouped by URL. The agent signs one acceptance, each Exchange returns a signed link, the agent fetches the page from your CDN with it, and your Exchange operator pays you."
    />

    <h3 class="sub-h" id="broker-order">How a Broker orders offers</h3>
    <p class="prose" set:html={`A FORA Broker orders offers by relevance to the agent's request (${a(src(7), 'Broker')}). For one URL, the Exchange shows every offer that agent may see. Offers without a scope are public. If the agent names a deal scope, the scoped offers come as well. If the agent filters for the kind of use, only the offers that fit it come back (${a(src(5, '#term-semantics'), 'Licensing terms')}). Nothing is decided for the agent: it decides what it buys, where and why.`} />
    <p class="prose" set:html={`Each offer arrives as its Exchange signed it, so a Broker can't change a price or a term (${a(src(7, '#offers-and-absences'), 'Broker')}). How a Broker ranks search results for relevance is its operator's to disclose.`} />

    <h3 class="sub-h">Your TollBit rates, shown to FORA agents <StatusTag status="design" /></h3>
    <p class="prose" set:html={`TollBit lets a platform act for a buyer: "When your platform makes a request to the TollBit API, you can supply the developer's credentials" (${a(src(28), 'Platform overview')}, §4.3). A FORA Broker would use that path with each agent's own TollBit account, and never buy or resell under one of its own. The agent is then the buyer and the account holder, so the content goes to no one else, as TollBit's license requires. A bridge Exchange would sign each TollBit rate as a FORA offer, marked as TollBit's terms, and the agent would fetch from TollBit.`} />

    <h3 class="sub-h">How other channels can use FORA</h3>
    <PointList items={SELL_THROUGH} />

    <Split aside="440px">
      <h3 class="sub-h">What FORA can't do with them</h3>
      <p class="prose" set:html={`<b>Settling a FORA sale through TollBit or Pay Per Crawl.</b> TollBit's API bills at TollBit's rates, and Pay Per Crawl charges when a crawler fetches a page. Neither one's docs describe a way to bill an amount for a sale made elsewhere.`} />
      <p class="prose" set:html={`<b>Answer engines' and Google's content as offers.</b> ProRata, Perplexity, Microsoft and Google license your content for their own products, and Microsoft for the partners it brings in. None offers it to any other agent.`} />
      <Figure
        slot="aside"
        src={`${IMG}/hub-5.png`}
        title="Where each channel meets FORA."
        caption={`Five places FORA meets the others; the Broker row is a design ${statusTag('design')}.`}
        alt="FORA meets the other channels at five layers: at your site's edge (beside TollBit in a rule order you write, as a Worker after Cloudflare's rules, always passing Googlebot, Bingbot and Applebot, never charging them); at the Broker (TollBit's rates under each agent's own TollBit account, Design); at the Exchange (marketplaces with their own catalog can run one); as an agent (a buyer that pays a share or a fee gets a free term and owes a usage report); and at settlement, outside the protocol, over x402 or Skyfire. Settling a FORA sale through TollBit or Pay Per Crawl, offering answer engines' and Google's content, and metering Googlebot don't fit."
      />
    </Split>
  </Band>

  <Band label="What to do" title="Before you sign with any of them">
    <PointList numbered items={TODO} />
    <Callout kind="legal" points={LEGAL} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="near" id="deep-pages" label="Deep pages">
    <ul class="deep">
      {DEEP.map((p) => (
        <li><a class="deep-card" href={p.href}><b>{p.title}</b><span>{p.summary}</span></a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" id="about">
    <Callout kind="about" points={ABOUT_FORA} columns />
    <section class="sources" id="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-label">
      <h2 class="end-label" id="sources-label">Sources</h2>
      <p class="note">Sources are checked on the date of publication.</p>
      <SourceList groups={SOURCES} compact numbered />
    </section>
    <SeriesNav current={HREF} />
  </Band>

  <ClosingCta
    title={TAGLINE}
    text="Sell to AI agents on your terms, or run your own marketplace. Start with what FORA takes on your stack, then register your domain on the publisher console. If you run a marketplace already, it can run on FORA too."
  >
    <a href="/getting-started/what-is-fora/" class="btn btn-primary">What is FORA?</a>
    <a href="/getting-started/publisher-onboarding/" class="btn btn-ghost">Publisher onboarding</a>
    <a href="/getting-started/existing-marketplace/" class="btn btn-ghost">For content marketplaces</a>
  </ClosingCta>
</LandingLayout>

<style>
  /* Prose between components, and the sub-headings that carry a StatusTag
     (the same few lines as on the TollBit page). */
  .prose { color: var(--lp-text); line-height: 1.65; max-width: 44rem; }
  .prose + .prose { margin-top: 1rem; }
  :global(*) + .prose { margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  .prose :global(a) { color: var(--lp-link); }
  .prose :global(b) { color: var(--lp-strong); font-weight: 600; }
  .prose :global(code) { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.875em; color: var(--lp-heading); overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .sub-h { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin: 3rem 0 0; color: var(--lp-heading); font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.01em; scroll-margin-top: 5rem; }
  .sub-h :global(.status-tag) { top: 0; }
  .sub-h + .prose { margin-top: 0.875rem; }

  /* Two define callouts side by side on a wide band. */
  .defs { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-top: 2.5rem; align-items: start; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .defs { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

  /* The one band lead with a link (What FORA takes). */
  :global(.band-lead a) { color: var(--lp-link); }
  .after-facts { margin-top: 2rem; font-size: 0.9375rem; color: var(--lp-muted); }
  .note-read { max-width: 52rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; font-size: 0.9375rem; }

  /* The closed disclosure under the map. */
  .more { margin-top: 2.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--lp-rule); padding-top: 1.25rem; }
  .more summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
